{
 "date": "2026-11-19 and 2026-11-20",
 "what": "The Strix Halo figures on the project's page: prompt reading speed with zest against the GPU alone, and how far its answers are from the GPU's. Two builds are in here. The 'speed' and 'accuracy' sections are the build of 2026-11-19 (the row split on by default, NPU weight copies limited to 20 GB by default). 'build_2026_11_20' is the build committed with this file, which differs in one thing: on Linux the default has no 20 GB limit, so Qwen3.8-27B gets all 64 layers on the NPU. 'page_rows' says which measurement each published row is.",
 "machine": "Ryzen AI MAX+ 395 / Radeon 8060S / XDNA2 NPU, 128 GB, Arch Linux (Omarchy), kernel 7.2.15 with its own amdxdna driver",
 "software": "llama.cpp b10944 Linux Vulkan release, unmodified; Mesa 26.2.5 (RADV); xrt 2.21.75-14, xrt-plugin-amdxdna 2.21.75-2; NPU power mode Default",
 "zest_build": {
  "libggml_zest_so_sha256_starts": "4806e80d6c326bda",
  "the_same_file_before_and_after_every_series": true,
  "source": "a87d2ae plus: results copied to the GPU by the GPU, pieces with no multiply run by the GPU, the row split on by default (GGML_XDNA_SPLIT=50). NPU weight copies limited to 20 GB by default."
 },
 "speed": {
  "method": "tools/bench.ps1's, run by a Python port of it (now tools/bench.py): per round one process, `zest llama-bench -m MODEL -dev Vulkan0,XDNA0/Vulkan0 -p 2048 -n 0 -ub 2048 -b 2048 -r 3 -o json`, so the model loads once and the GPU-alone and with-zest figures sit next to each other in time; a round's ratio compares that pair; the row gives the median over the rounds and the slowest and fastest round. 'split_off' is a second process per round with GGML_XDNA_SPLIT=0 (the NPU takes whole pieces and the GPU waits its turn), with a GPU-alone figure of its own.",
  "conditions": "Each model in a turn of its own with the machine reserved (the machine's scheduler holds other sessions' commands for the run and reports whether any outside process used more than half a core: CLEAN for every row). GPU load and load average at the start of each are in the rows.",
